    "result_text": "The build succeeds and all tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented disposable-domain email rejection in the owner-create flow.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.java` (the single `POST /api/owners` handler):\n\n1. Added a blocklist constant:\n   ```java\n   private static final java.util.Set<String> DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS =\n       java.util.Set.of(\"mailinator.com\", \"tempmail.com\", \"guerrillamail.com\");\n   ```\n\n2. Extended `normalizeEmail(...)` \u2014 which already runs during create \u2014 so that after the existing syntactic validation and lower-casing, it extracts the domain (the part after `@`) and rejects it if present on the blocklist:\n   ```java\n   String domain = normalized.substring(normalized.indexOf('@') + 1);\n   if (DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS.contains(domain)) {\n       throw new InvalidOwnerFieldsException(List.of(\"email\"));\n   }\n   ```\n\nI reused the existing `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`, whose handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice` already returns **400 Bad Request** (listing `email` in the `errors` array) \u2014 consistent with how other invalid-field business rules are handled. The domain comparison is case-insensitive since it's done against the already-lower-cased value. I also updated the method's Javadoc to document the new rule.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsDisposableDomain` (posting `x@mailinator.com` and expecting 400) passes, as does the full suite.",
